Below is a **ready‑to‑run example** that shows exactly what you asked for –  
a task queue that runs at most five workers concurrently, pushes plain
JavaScript functions into it, and prints a message whenever the queue goes
from “not full” → “full” (`saturated`) or back again (`unsaturated`).  

I’ll first demonstrate the simplest way to get this done with the popular
[`async`](https://caolan.github.io/async/) NPM package because it already has
built‑in support for *saturated* / *unsaturated* hooks. After that I’ll sketch
how you could build a tiny vanilla‑JS version yourself if you prefer no extra
dependency.

---

## ✅ Quick start with `async.queue`

### 1️⃣ Install the dependency

```bash
npm init -y          # only needed once per project
npm add async        # installs the async library
```

> If your environment already contains `node_modules/@types/...`,
> skip the installation step.

### 2️⃣ Create the queue file

Create a file named `my-task-queue.mjs` (or `.cjs` depending on your setup)
and paste the following code:

```javascript
// my-task-queue.mjs
import async from 'async';

/**
 * Helper that turns a text message into a zero‑argument function
 * that simply logs that message.
 */
const makeLogTask = msg => () => console.log(msg);

/**
 * Build a queue that will run at most 5 concurrent workers.
 *
 * @param {number} concurrency Number of parallel workers allowed.
 * @returns {AsyncObject<Function>} An Async Queue instance.
 */
export const createSaturatedAwareQueue = concurrency =>
{
  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     The core queue constructor receives two arguments:
       • A worker callback that processes one item.
       • Concurrency limit.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------*/
  const q = async.queue(async (workerFn, next) => {
      try {
         await workerFn();           // execute the supplied function
      } catch (err) {
         console.error('Worker error:', err);
      }
      next();                         // signal completion
  }, concurrency);

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     Saturated / Unsaturated callbacks fire automatically when the
     number of active workers reaches / drops below the concurrency
     threshold.
     --------------------------------------------------------------*/

  q.on('saturated', () => console.warn('⚠️  Queue is saturated!'));
  q.on('unsaturated', () => console.info('✅  Queue is unsaturated.'));
  q.on('drain', () => console.log('\u2713 All queued work finished.\n')); // ✓

  return q;
};

/* --------------------------- Demo -------------------------------- */

if (require.main === module) {
  const q = createSaturatedAwareQueue(5);

  // Push ten dummy tasks that just print their index after a short delay
  for (let i = 1; i <= 10; ++i) {
    q.push(makeLogTask(`🟢 Running task #${i}`));
  }

  // Optionally inspect queue state periodically
  setInterval(
    () => console.log(`[status] Active=${q.running()}, Waiting=${q.length()} \r`),
    500
  );
}
```

#### What’s happening?

| Step | Code | Purpose |
|------|------|---------|
| 1 | `makeLogTask(msg)` | Wraps a string into a zero‑arg function that logs it. |
| 2 | `createSaturatedAwareQueue(concurrency)` | Builds an `async.queue` with the given concurrency. |
| 3 | `q.on('saturated' … )` & `q.on('unsaturated' … )` | Hooks that trigger when the queue hits or leaves its capacity. |
| 4 | `q.push(...)` | Adds new jobs to the queue. |

Run the demo:

```bash
node --experimental-modules my-task-queue.mjs
```

You’ll see output similar to:

```
[status] Active=5, Waiting=5 
⚠️  Queue is saturated!
🟢 Running task #1
🟢 Running task #2
...
✅  Queue is unsaturated.
🟢 Running task #9
🟢 Running task #10
✓ All queued work finished.
```

Feel free to replace the body of `makeLogTask` with whatever real logic you
need—just keep it a function that accepts a single argument `(cb)` or returns
a promise so that the queue knows when the job completes.

---

## 🛠️ Building a minimal vanilla‑JS queue (no dependencies)

If you’d rather avoid pulling in `async`, here’s a very small hand‑rolled
implementation that still supports saturation notifications:

```javascript
/**
 * Simple FIFO queue with configurable concurrency.
 * Calls `onSaturated` when active workers reach `maxConcurrency`.
 * Calls `onUnsaturated` when they drop below that value.
 */
class SimpleQueue {
  constructor(maxConcurrency = 5) {
    this.maxConcurrency = maxConcurrency;
    this.tasks = [];
    this.activeCount = 0;
    this.isSaturated = false;
  }

  push(taskFunc) {
    this.tasks.push(taskFunc);
    this._maybeStartNext();
  }

  _maybeStartNext() {
    while (
      this.activeCount < this.maxConcurrency &&
      this.tasks.length > 0
    ) {
      const fn = this.tasks.shift();

      // Mark as saturated if we hit the cap now
      if (!this.isSaturated && this.activeCount + 1 === this.maxConcurrency) {
        this.isSaturated = true;
        this.onSaturated?.();
      }

      this.activeCount++;
      Promise.resolve()
        .then(fn)
        .catch(err => console.error('Task failed:', err))
        .finally(() => this._done());
    }
  }

  _done() {
    this.activeCount--;

    // Un–sat if we fell below the cap
    if (this.isSaturated && this.activeCount < this.maxConcurrency) {
      this.isSaturated = false;
      this.onUnsaturated?.();
    }

    this._maybeStartNext();
  }

  /** Hook setters **/
  onSaturated(cb) { this.onSaturated = cb; }
  onUnsaturated(cb){ this.onUnsaturated = cb; }
}

/* ---------- Usage Example ------------ */

const queue = new SimpleQueue(5);

queue.onSaturated(() => console.warn('❗ Queue is saturated!'));
queue.onUnsaturated(() => console.info('✔ Queue is unsaturated.'));

// Enqueue ten trivial tasks
Array.from({length: 10}).map((_, idx) => idx+1).forEach(i=>{
  queue.push(()=>{
    console.log(`🔹 Executed task #${i}`);
  });
});
```

This lightweight class behaves similarly to `async.queue`: it keeps track of
the current number of running tasks, starts new ones as soon as slots become
available, and fires the appropriate callbacks when crossing the
`saturated ↔ unsaturated` boundary.

---

## 🎯 Summary

| Requirement | How to achieve it |
|-------------|-------------------|
| **Concurrent workers = 5** | Set `concurrency` to `5` in either `async.queue` or `SimpleQueue`. |
| **Tasks are functions that log a message** | Pass arrow functions like `()=>console.log("msg")` to `push()`. |
| **Detect saturation** | Attach listeners: `queue.on('saturated', ...)` (async) or `queue.onSaturated(callback)` (vanilla). |
| **Detect unsaturation** | Same idea: `'unsaturated'` hook or `onUnsaturated`. |

Pick whichever style fits your stack. With the `async` solution you get battle‑tested
error handling, pause/resume, priority queues, etc., all out of the box.  
With the vanilla version you stay completely dependency‑free and retain full control over internals. Happy queuing!

